WHAT WAS WRONG. Several gates report on one commit -- push/woodpecker beside
cron/full-suite -- and each is a SEPARATE Woodpecker pipeline with its own
target_url. _github_fetch_job_log took the FIRST status carrying a target_url
and broke out of the loop. The job name was applied only later, to choose a
STEP inside a pipeline that had already been chosen wrongly. So every job name
returned the push transcript, which is exactly what the item recorded: five
different job names, one identical "396 passed" output.

The job name was being asked the wrong question. It selected within a
pipeline; nothing selected the pipeline.

TWO RULES, in order:
  1. an explicit job name selects its OWN gate, matched on the full context
     or its trailing segment -- operators type "full-suite", not
     "ci/woodpecker/cron/full-suite";
  2. with no name, or a name matching nothing, prefer the gate that FAILED.
     Reaching for a log means something broke, so defaulting to the first
     status handed back the GREEN pipeline while a different gate was red --
     the same wrong answer, reached without even a typo to blame. This
     mirrors the step-level rule already in the file five lines below.

MEASURED, on dev 45280d90 (push success + cron/full-suite failure):
  before  ci-debug logs cron/full-suite -> the PUSH transcript, "396 passed"
  after   ci-debug logs cron/full-suite -> the cron transcript, 174 lines,
          ending "3 failed, 1498 passed, 2 skipped"
Five unit tests pin it, including a control asserting the single-gate case is
byte-identical to before -- the change must be strictly more capable, never
less, which is the same contract WI-solob's original credential path shipped
under.

